p('How Often Must the Cap Return?','Title');p('A fixed-cap timing frontier after a one-time handover loses protection','Center');p('Mohammadpayam Teabi | 26 October 2026','Center')
h('1 Hold the cap fixed; change its timing')
p('Two shops choose prices simultaneously. Between cap rounds, a guard allows any price whose modeled immediate profit against the previous rival price is within epsilon of the best profit. A real cap execution instead permits the full price interval up to the original physical cap, independently of old prices. It replaces the guard on that execution; it is not their intersection. A cap is applied immediately and then every K executions, with K-1 ordinary guard executions between caps.')
p('The preceding transfer study found that a finite initialization cannot protect a target above the unchanged guard\'s continuous worst long-run floor. We now test an ongoing intervention: the SAME inherited physical cap. Eight original labels, five unchanged targets and two tolerance semantics are fixed before the run (80 comparisons). The 90/95/99/100% labels refer to each original permanent-cap surplus floor; the anchor label uses the certified lower bound of the old near-Nash anchor. Requested values are relaxed by their saved allowance 1e-8*max(1,requested value); all comparisons retain the resulting saved thresholds. These labels include equivalent calibrations; they are not independent economic replications.')
p(f'<b>Base example.</b> For the original 95% target minus margin, F approximately {float(target["actualThresholdExact"]):.6f}, a cap every 4 executions guarantees approximately {mid(target["safePeriod"]["averageConsumerSurplus"]):.6f} customer surplus on average. Every 5 is insufficient: its attained worst average is approximately {mid(target["nextFailingPeriod"]["averageConsumerSurplus"]):.6f}. This is maximality within the fixed-cap, fixed-period family, not over all controllers.')
fig('base-timing-frontier.png','Figure1. Validated effective-continuum values, shown approximately. The cap ceiling and economic parameters stay fixed. A cycle-average guarantee differs from its worst individual round; the latter can fall below the protected average.')
p('<b>The negative result matters.</b> The 99% and 100% target labels require a cap every execution in all eight labels. Seven 90% targets already hold without recurring intervention; the doubled-tolerance label requires K=17. Recurrence is not universally light-touch.','Small')
br()
h('2 Why the worst cycle is exact')
p('Write normalized prices x=(p-c)/mu. The inherited continuous analysis validates a nondecreasing upper permitted-price map U with contraction factor rho&lt;1 and unique fixed point q_star. Customer surplus C falls in either price. Normalize the inherited physical cap as A=(p_cap-c)/mu and verify ell&lt;=A&lt;q_star. Then a_0=A, a_(j+1)=U(a_j) increases to q_star, and r_j=C(a_j,a_j) decreases to C_star.')
p('On a cap round, every permitted pair is at most(A,A). By induction and monotonicity, every permitted pair at phase j is at most(a_j,a_j). Thus its customer surplus is at least r_j; selecting both upper allowed prices attains equality. This proves an all-permitted-policy guarantee and an attaining adverse path. It does not assert that independent sellers choose that path.')
p('G_K = (r_0 + ... + r_(K-1))/K<br/>Worst cumulative CS at T=m*K+s:<br/>m*(r_0 + ... + r_(K-1)) + (r_0 + ... + r_(s-1))','Equation')
p('Because the rewards descend, EVERY finite average measured from the first actual cap execution is at least G_K, as is the long-run average. An old price pair earns no reward. Observation starting at an arbitrary phase has only the long-run guarantee unless the initial partial cycle is separately bounded. A single round can fall below G_K.')
fig('activation-prefix.png','Figure2. The attained worst path for the base effective K=4 controller. Green bands mark actual cap executions. Cumulative averages begin with the rewarded cap, not an artificial initial-state observation.',width=493)
p('G_K strictly decreases to C_star. The largest safe integer requires both G_K.lower&gt;=F and G_(K+1).upper&lt;F. We compute 64 outward-bounded orbit terms using separate signed endpoint solves, then use a contraction/Lipschitz geometric tail. The declared search ceiling is 65536; a safe ceiling would remain censored rather than imply an evaluated 65537. No case was censored or unresolved here. The nominal epsilon and primary effective exact-real epsilon+tau (imported tau=1e-12) yield the same integer classifications. These continuous semantics do not exactly replay every binary64 profit operation.','Small')
br()
h('3 Keep every target, including unhelpful outcomes')
fig('fixed-target-periods.png','Figure3. Every inherited scenario/target label is retained. Entries are largest certified periods, not learned choices. None means the unchanged guard already meets the long-run target: zero startup caps suffice for that objective, whereas one immediate cap additionally protects all finite activation averages. Physical cap prices can differ across original grid-size labels; cross-label timing differences cannot be attributed solely to resolution.',width=493)
h('4 A separately certified, playable numerical game')
p('The graphical game uses 61 base quotes and prioritizes the EXACT inherited cap over any base quote within the predeclared 1e-12 absolute distance, then sorts and deduplicates (61/62 quotes). Its profit comparisons and allowed masks follow the specified binary64 calculation. Saved customer-surplus weights are then treated as exact rational numbers. Full-table monotonicity and the upper-map conditions are checked separately. Its certificate is for that numerical finite game, not an exact-real continuous logit certificate.')
p('For a general finite cycle the all-activation-average floor is H_K=min_(1&lt;=s&lt;=K)(r_0+...+r_(s-1))/s. This also handles cycles that do not descend. The saved cases independently verify descending rewards, so H_K=G_K here. The game exposes both shops\' permitted prices, expected purchases, individual profits, cap clock, actual executions, undo/reset and separate manual/scripted ledgers. The scripted maximum-price benchmark uses no model player and is not a profit-maximizing strategy.')
p(f'<b>Independent checks.</b> Continuous audit: {ca["records"]} models, {ca["targetLabels"]} target labels, {ca["scalarRootCertificates"]:,} scalar root certificates and {ca["checks"]:,} checks; five corruption controls rejected. Finite audit: {fa["payoffPairs"]:,} payoff pairs, {fa["checks"]:,} checks and {fa["executionsChecked"]:,} actual executions; four controls rejected. Independent Fraction code verifies all {ra["certificates"]} finite cycle/prefix certificates and rejects three corruptions. Controller logic and 130-digit interval code are separate from the primary 80-digit implementation; both interval stacks share Python Decimal transcendental arithmetic. This is not formal verification or independent arithmetic-library validation.','Small')
